<title>BELGIUM: Greenpeace welcomes maize ban.</title>
<headline>Greenpeace welcomes maize ban.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-12-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Environmental group Greenpeace welcomed on Monday an Austrian government ban on imports of genetically modified U.S. maize developed by Swiss Ciba-Geigy.</p>
<p>&quot;Austria has given the lead to other member states (of the European Union)...Greenpeace urges them to follow the Austrian example and challenge the Commission's decision,&quot; campaigner Michelle Sheathert said in a statement.</p>
<p>Following scientific advice, the European Commission last week approved the sale of the maize with as-yet unspecified labelling conditions, although only France and Spain had supported the move.</p>
<p>Austrian Health Minister Christa Krammer told Austrian radio that the government will ban imports from early January because insufficient work had been done on the antibiotic gene in the maize.</p>
<p>The maize, which will be imported from the United States, carries an antibiotic &quot;marker gene&quot; and has been genetically modified to resist a herbicide and the corn borer pest.</p>
<p>Under EU rules, member states can challenge the Commission's decision if they think it poses a risk to human health or to the environment.</p>
</text>
